Bay bundles xdg-settings and xdg-mime beside the Linux browser executable.
Helium uses the same two-helper runtime layout:
https://github.com/imputnet/helium-linux/blob/473c0f72d7db2e8492f0fa975b8544bc719bb027/scripts/package.sh

Source: Chromium's official xdg-utils dependency repository
https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/deps/xdg-utils/
Revision: f16d51e9ce95f4173ba878a61b59317f73005562
License: MIT, see LICENSE and the individual scripts' copyright notices.
The checked-in scripts are generated upstream, ready to run without a build.
README.chromium is the upstream attribution, retained without changes.

Upstream files, relative to that revision:
scripts/xdg-settings
scripts/xdg-mime
LICENSE
README.chromium

Bay changes: the three xdg-mime invocations in xdg-settings explicitly use
"$(dirname "$0")/xdg-mime". This keeps both checks and setters on the bundled
helper, including when /opt/bay is absent from PATH. No other script changes.
The Chromium patch chromium-bay-xdg-settings-linux.patch makes setting and
output-capturing checks prefer the xdg-settings sibling of the browser binary.

